# Template for the project root's .htaccess — copy it into place on deploy:
#
#     cp .htaccess.example .htaccess
#
# A tripwire, and on a correct deployment a dead file.
#
# The document root is public/ (E7.3, #141). Apache reads .htaccess from the
# document root downward and never above it, so if the vhost is pointed where
# it should be, nothing in here is ever consulted. It exists for the deployment
# where that is not true: a docroot left at the repository root, a new server
# set up from memory, a panel that reset it.
#
# In that case every request here is refused and the site is down. That is the
# intended outcome. The alternative is the one this project actually shipped
# for a while -- .env, composer.lock and every .php under src/ and tests/
# addressable, the last of them executed by the engine on request (E7, #138).
# A site that is off announces itself in a minute; a site that is serving its
# own source does not announce itself at all.
#
# So if you are reading this because the site returned 403: the fix is the
# document root, not this file. Point the vhost at .../fh-trip-builder/public
# and copy public/.htaccess.example into place there.
#
# If the host will not let you move the document root, replace the refusal
# below with a rewrite into public/, which reaches the same place by a weaker
# route -- the root stays servable and correctness depends on this rule rather
# than on the files being elsewhere:
#
#     RewriteEngine On
#     RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/public/
#     RewriteRule ^(.*)$ public/$1 [L]
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteRule ^ - [F]
</IfModule>
